::SPEECH_U04_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U04_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.02
Question:
Source: Clear supporting details help listeners follow a speech. Which detail supports that central idea?
{
~A. Use a random detail from the source#This choice is too weak because it does not connect source structure, evidence, or central idea to the speech task.
~B. Choose a detail only because it is short#This choice may mention the topic, but it misses how the source supports clear communication for an audience.
=C. A speech that jumps between unrelated ideas can cause listeners to miss the purpose#Correct. This choice uses the source accurately and connects the source information to research-supported speech communication.
~D. Use evidence without explaining the connection#This choice does not give the speaker a reliable way to use source information in a speech.
}
::SPEECH_U04_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U04_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.02
Question:
Which choice best identifies central idea support?
{
=A. A detail supports the central idea when it explains or develops that main message#Correct. This choice uses the source accurately and connects the source information to research-supported speech communication.
~B. Choose a detail only because it is short#This choice may mention the topic, but it misses how the source supports clear communication for an audience.
~C. Ignore the source central idea#This choice reflects a misconception. Check the source purpose, central idea, and support before choosing evidence.
~D. Use evidence without explaining the connection#This choice does not give the speaker a reliable way to use source information in a speech.
}
